.TH MU2E-POWER-NETCHECK 1 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-power-netcheck \- phase 3: inter-node connectivity across the DAQ networks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-power-netcheck
.RB [ \-\-include\-failed ]
.RB [ \-\-location
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-node
.IR HOST ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs phase 3 of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1).
.PP
Phases 1 and 2 establish that each node is reachable
.I from a gateway.
That is a weaker property than the nodes being able to reach
.IR "each other" .
After a power event a switch can come back with a VLAN missing, a port in the
wrong group, or jumbo frames disabled on one uplink, and every node still
passes its own health checks while the DAQ cannot move data. This phase probes
node to node.
.PP
The networks probed, and how, come from the
.I mesh:
section of
.BR mu2edaq-checks.yaml (5):
.TP
.B data
Full mesh, with a jumbo-frame probe. This is the network the DAQ actually uses
and it is small enough to afford O(N^2) probes.
.TP
.BR lab ", " ipmi
Sampled: each node is probed against a small set of anchor hosts, which is O(N)
and sufficient to find a segment that has not come back.
.PP
Probes for one source are packed into a single remote shell invocation rather
than one SSH session per pair \(em the shipped topology has 49 nodes on the data
network, so the mesh is 2352 ordered pairs, and doing that as 2352 sessions
would take longer than the rest of the recovery. As one session per source it
is 49.
.PP
The results are interpreted, not merely tabulated. A node that reached nothing
is reported separately from a node that nobody could reach: the first is a node
or switch-port problem, the second suggests a one-way path, an ARP problem or a
host firewall, and they need different responses.
.PP
The probes run on the nodes, so they use those nodes' own
.BR ping (8)
dialect. The jumbo-frame probe uses the iputils-only
.BR \-M " and " \-s
options and is valid because this phase always executes on a gateway, never on
the operator's workstation.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
is accepted except
.BR \-\-phase ,
which is fixed to
.BR network .
.SS Phase 3 control
.TP
.B \-\-include\-failed
Probe nodes that failed an earlier phase. By default they are skipped, because
probing a node already known to be down costs a full timeout per pair and tells
the operator nothing new.
.SS Run control
.TP
.BI \-\-label " TEXT"
Label for this run, shown in the report header and the logbook subject.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Accepted, and has no effect: this phase issues no power command.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Contact nothing at all; answer every command from the built-in script.
.TP
.BI \-\-location " NAME"
Limit the probe to one location:
.BR mc2 ", " mc1 " or " teststand .
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-node " HOST"
Limit the probe to these nodes. Repeatable; short or fully-qualified names are
both accepted. The mesh is then built over the named nodes only, which is the
quick way to test one suspect path.
.TP
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
Accepted; it governs phase 2 and has no effect here.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " STAGE" ", " \-\-until " STAGE"
Accepted; they bound the phase-2 power-on sequence and have no effect here.
.SS Credentials
.TP
.BI \-\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal for ordinary logins. The probes are issued over SSH, so this
phase needs a working login to every source node.
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal with root access on the nodes.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prompt
Never prompt for a password; fail with instructions instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-vault\-addr " URL"
HashiCorp Vault address.
.SS Report
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Where the report pages are written. Default:
.IR html/ .
.TP
.B \-\-publish
Copy the report to
.I report.publish.target
afterwards.
.TP
.BI \-\-publish\-target " DEST"
Override the publication target. Implies
.BR \-\-publish .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-report
Do not write the HTML pages; console output only.
.TP
.B \-\-post\-ecl
Accepted; the logbook entry is written by phase 4. See
.BR mu2e-power-report (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-run\-id " N"
Accepted; only phase 4 reads it.
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.I mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Main configuration file. Default:
.IR config/power-recovery.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Dotenv file. Default:
.IR config/.env .
.TP
.BI \-\-database\-url " URL"
SQLAlchemy URL for the run store.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-self\-update
Skip the phase-0 check for a newer revision.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-checks
Print every registered check and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-nodes
Print the node inventory and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the mesh results as JSON on stdout instead of tables.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Debug logging, including one line per credential attempted against each source
node.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Warnings and errors only.
.SH NOTES
This phase can be the first thing a session runs, which means it can start with
no gateway session cached. Gateway selection is serialised for that reason: the
first worker thread that needs a gateway probes for one and the rest wait,
rather than sixteen threads each opening a TCP sweep and a full SSH handshake
against the same two hosts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every probed path works.
.TP
.B 1
At least one path failed, or a path could not carry a full jumbo frame.
.TP
.B 2
The probe could not start.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-netcheck
mu2e-power-netcheck \-\-location teststand
mu2e-power-netcheck \-\-include\-failed \-v
mu2e-power-netcheck \-\-node mu2e-trk-03 \-\-node mu2e-dl-01 \-\-json
.EE
.RE
